TypeScript and Duck Typing are two distinct concepts in software development, and while they are related, they serve different purposes.

TypeScript is a statically-typed superset of JavaScript that provides type annotations, interfaces, and classes to improve the development experience and help catch errors at compile-time. TypeScript is a tool that compiles to JavaScript, which means that it can be used to write code that runs in a variety of environments, including web browsers, Node.js, and the browser runtime.

Duck Typing is a design pattern in object-oriented programming, which is a way to check if an object can be used in a certain context without checking its type. It's based on the principle of "If it looks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's a duck." In other words, an object can be used as a substitute for another object if it has the same properties and methods, regardless of its actual type.

TypeScript and Duck Typing have different purposes, but they can be used together in certain scenarios.

TypeScript provides type annotations, interfaces, and classes to improve the development experience and help catch errors at compile-time. Type annotations help ensure that your code is more robust, maintainable, and easier to read. Interfaces define a set of properties, methods, and events that an object must have to conform to a particular type, while classes provide a blueprint for creating objects with specific properties and methods.

Duck Typing, on the other hand, focuses on the behavior of an object, rather than its type. This allows objects with different types to be used interchangeably, as long as they have the same methods and properties.

TypeScript can be used to implement Duck Typing by leveraging its type system. By defining interfaces or abstract classes, you can enforce a specific set of methods and properties that an object must have to conform to a particular type. If an object meets the requirements of the interface or abstract class, it can be used as a substitute for an object of that type.

Here's an example of how TypeScript can be used to implement Duck Typing:

```typescript
interface Animal {
  makeSound(): void;
}

class Duck implements Animal {
  makeSound(): void {
    console.log("Quack!");
  }
}

class Cat implements Animal {
  makeSound(): void {
    console.log("Meow!");
  }
}

function makeAnimalSound(animal: Animal) {
  animal.makeSound();
}

// Both Duck and Cat can be passed to makeAnimalSound
makeAnimalSound(new Duck()); // "Quack!"
makeAnimalSound(new Cat()); // "Meow!"
```

In this example, the `Animal` interface defines a `makeSound()` method, which is implemented by both `Duck` and `Cat`. The `makeAnimalSound` function takes an `Animal` parameter, which means that it can be passed any object that conforms to the `Animal` interface, regardless of its actual type. Both `Duck` and `Cat` objects can be used interchangeably in this scenario because they both have the same `makeSound()` method, even though they are different classes.

In summary, TypeScript provides a type system to improve the development experience, while Duck Typing is a design pattern that focuses on behavior. By leveraging TypeScript's type system, you can enforce a specific set of methods and properties that an object must have to conform to a particular type, allowing for Duck Typing in your code.

Before starting, make sure you have a Discord bot account and have installed and set up `discord.js` version 14. You will also need to create a new Discord application, enable the bot in the application, and add the bot to the server where you want to use it.

Here's an example of a Discord bot using Discord.js v14 with a `ban` command:

```javascript
// Import the necessary Discord.js modules
const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, SlashCommandBuilder, PermissionFlagsBits } = require('discord.js');

// Create a new Discord client
const client = new Client({
  intents: [
    GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
    GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
    GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
    GatewayIntentBits.DirectMessages,
  ],
});

// Define the command builder for the ban command
const banCommand = new SlashCommandBuilder()
  .setName('ban')
  .setDescription('Bans a user from the server')
  .addUserOption((option) =>
    option
      .setName('user')
      .setDescription('The user to ban')
      .setRequired(true)
  )
  .addStringOption((option) =>
    option
      .setName('reason')
      .setDescription('The reason for the ban')
      .setRequired(false)
  )
  .setDefaultMemberPermissions(PermissionFlagsBits.BanMembers);

// Define the command handler for the ban command
client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
  if (!interaction.isCommand()) return;

  if (interaction.commandName === 'ban') {
    const { member, options } = interaction;

    // Get the user to ban
    const userToBan = options.getUser('user');

    // Get the ban reason
    const reason = options.getString('reason') || 'No reason provided';

    // Check if the user has the necessary permissions
    if (!member.permissions.has(PermissionFlagsBits.BanMembers)) {
      return interaction.reply({
        content: 'You do not have the necessary permissions to ban users.',
        ephemeral: true,
      });
    }

    // Check if the user to ban is the bot
    if (userToBan.id === client.user.id) {
      return interaction.reply({
        content: 'I can\'t ban myself. That would be weird.',
        ephemeral: true,
      });
    }

    // Check if the user to ban is the bot owner
    if (userToBan.id === process.env.OWNER_ID) {
      return interaction.reply({
        content: 'I can\'t ban the bot owner. That would be rude.',
        ephemeral: true,
      });
    }

    // Ban the user
    try {
      await interaction.guild.members.ban(userToBan, { days: 7, reason: reason });

      // Send a success message
      return interaction.reply({
        content: `Successfully banned ${userToBan.user.tag} for ${reason}`,
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      return interaction.reply({
        content: 'An error occurred while trying to ban the user.',
        ephemeral: true,
      });
    }
  }
});

// Log in the client and listen for ready events
client.login(process.env.TOKEN).then(() => {
  console.log('Bot logged in and ready!');

  client.on('ready', () => {
    console.log(`Logged in as ${client.user.tag}`);

    // Register the ban command
    client.application.commands.create({
      name: 'ban',
      description: 'Bans a user from the server',
      options: banCommand.toJSON(),
    });
  });
});
```

Note: Make sure to replace the `process.env.TOKEN` with your Discord bot's token, and `process.env.OWNER_ID` with the ID of the Discord user who owns the bot.

This code should create a Discord bot with a `ban` slash command. When a user executes the command, the bot will ban the user specified in the `user` option, with the reason provided in the `reason` option (or a default reason of "No reason provided" if the reason option is not specified). The bot will also check if the user executing the command has the necessary permissions to ban users, and if the user to ban is the bot, the bot owner, or the bot itself. If the ban is successful, the bot will send a message to the user executing the command indicating that the ban was successful. If an error occurs, the bot will send an error message to the user executing the command.

